<main>

<section>
 <h2>3-month trend — headline &amp; core inflation</h2>
 <p class="subnote">All figures are 12-month (year-over-year) percent change, not seasonally adjusted, from BLS CPI news releases.</p>
 <table class="trend-table card">
 <tr>
 <th>Measure</th><th>April 2026</th><th>May 2026</th><th>June 2026</th><th>Trend</th>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">CPI-U, all items (headline)</td>
 <td>3.8%</td><td>4.2%</td><td>3.5%</td>
 <td class="arrow-down">↓ pulled back</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">Core CPI (excl. food &amp; energy)</td>
 <td>2.8%</td><td>2.9%</td><td>2.6%</td>
 <td class="arrow-down">↓ eased</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">Food at home (groceries)</td>
 <td>2.9%</td><td>2.7%</td><td>2.7%</td>
 <td class="arrow-flat">↔ steady</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">Medical care (CPI)</td>
 <td>2.5%</td><td>2.6%</td><td>2.0%</td>
 <td class="arrow-down">↓ cooling</td>
 </tr>
 </table>
 <div class="note card" style="margin-top:12px;">
 June brought the first deceleration in four months, and it was a big one: headline CPI fell from 4.2% to 3.5% — the largest one-month drop in the index itself since April 2020. It's almost entirely an energy story, not a broad cooldown: a ceasefire reopened the Strait of Hormuz, gasoline fell 9.7% for the month on a seasonally adjusted basis (still up 26.7% year-over-year), and energy overall swung from +23.5% to +15.7%. Core CPI also eased, from 2.9% to 2.6%, but Fed Chair Kevin Warsh cautioned today that this one month shouldn't be read as "mission accomplished" — shelter and services inflation remain sticky underneath the energy swing.
 </div>
</section>

<section>
 <h2>Metrics outside normal variance range</h2>
 <p class="subnote">BLS publishes a standard error and a "largest/smallest change since" comparison for every CPI line each month — the flags below are pulled directly from that published context, not an outside estimate.</p>
 <ul class="flag-list card" style="padding:4px 16px;">
 <li>
 <span class="fl-metric">Headline CPI, monthly (SA): -0.4%</span>
 <span class="fl-why">Largest 1-month decrease since April 2020 (-0.8%). BLS CPI Table 6.</span>
 </li>
 <li>
 <span class="fl-metric">Energy index, monthly (SA): -5.7%</span>
 <span class="fl-why">Largest 1-month decline since April 2020. BLS CPI Table 6.</span>
 </li>
 <li>
 <span class="fl-metric">Gasoline, monthly (SA): -9.7%</span>
 <span class="fl-why">Largest 1-month drop since August 2022. BLS CPI Table 6.</span>
 </li>
 <li>
 <span class="fl-metric">Eggs, monthly (SA): +4.3%</span>
 <span class="fl-why">Largest 1-month increase since February 2025; eggs are flagged by BLS as one of its most volatile tracked items even outside crisis periods. BLS CPI Table 6.</span>
 </li>
 <li>
 <span class="fl-metric">Shelter, monthly (SA): +0.1%</span>
 <span class="fl-why">Smallest 1-month increase since January 2021 — a meaningful deceleration in the CPI's largest single component. BLS CPI Table 6.</span>
 </li>
 <li>
 <span class="fl-metric">Motor vehicle insurance, monthly (SA): -2.0%</span>
 <span class="fl-why">Largest 1-month decline since October 2020. BLS CPI Table 6.</span>
 </li>
 <li>
 <span class="fl-metric">Nonfarm payroll growth: +57,000 in June</span>
 <span class="fl-why">Down from +148,000 in April; April and May were also revised down by a combined 74,000 — a sharper slowdown than the recent trend. BLS Employment Situation.</span>
 </li>
 <li>
 <span class="fl-metric">Labor force participation: 61.5%</span>
 <span class="fl-why">Lowest reading since March 2021. BLS Employment Situation.</span>
 </li>
 </ul>
</section>

<section>
 <h2>Groceries (food at home)</h2>
 <div class="stat-grid">
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Grocery CPI, 12-mo</div>
 <div class="value up">2.7%</div>
 <div class="period">June 2026 vs. June 2025</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Monthly change</div>
 <div class="value flat">+0.2%</div>
 <div class="period">May → June 2026</div>
 </div>
 </div>

 <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">Top 5 grocery items by 12-month price swing</h3>
 <p class="subnote">The five individual grocery items moving furthest from "normal," up or down, from BLS CPI Table 2, June 2026.</p>
 <div class="stat-grid">
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Coffee, 12-mo</div>
 <div class="value up">+12.9%</div>
 <div class="period">-2.0% for the month alone</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Uncooked ground beef, 12-mo</div>
 <div class="value up">+12.4%</div>
 <div class="period">beef &amp; veal overall: +11.8%</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Milk (fresh whole), 12-mo</div>
 <div class="value up">+9.0%</div>
 <div class="period">+3.3% for the month alone</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Chicken, 12-mo</div>
 <div class="value down">-2.3%</div>
 <div class="period">one of the few grocery items falling</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Eggs, 12-mo</div>
 <div class="value down">-27.9%</div>
 <div class="period">still recovering from 2025 spike</div>
 <span class="flag">Outside normal range</span>
 </div>
 </div>

 <div class="note card" style="margin-top:12px;">
 Groceries held basically flat in June: food-at-home prices rose 2.7% year-over-year, the same pace as May, with 4 of the 6 major grocery-store food groups increasing. Food away from home (restaurants) is running hotter, up 3.4% year-over-year. Beef and coffee are the standout increases — ground beef is up double digits as cattle herds stay historically small, and coffee is still working through last year's global supply shock. Eggs remain the dashboard's most volatile line: prices are down sharply from last year's avian-flu-driven spike, but June alone saw a 4.3% monthly jump, the largest one-month egg price move since February 2025 — BLS itself has flagged eggs as one of its most volatile tracked items even before the pandemic.
 </div>
 <div class="source-card card" style="margin-top:12px;">
