<section>
 <h2>Housing costs (shelter CPI)</h2>
 <p class="subnote">12-month percent change, not seasonally adjusted, from BLS CPI news releases.</p>
 <table class="trend-table card">
 <tr>
 <th>Measure</th><th>April 2026</th><th>May 2026</th><th>June 2026</th><th>Trend</th>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">Shelter (overall)</td>
 <td>3.3%</td><td>3.4%</td><td>3.3%</td>
 <td class="arrow-flat">↔ holding near 3.3%</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">Rent of primary residence</td>
 <td>—</td><td>up y/y</td><td>up y/y</td>
 <td class="arrow-up">↑ rising</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">Owners' equivalent rent (OER)</td>
 <td>—</td><td>up y/y</td><td>up y/y</td>
 <td class="arrow-up">↑ rising</td>
 </tr>
 </table>
 <div class="note card" style="margin-top:12px;">
 Shelter is the single largest component of the CPI basket (about a third of the index), so it barely budged even while headline CPI swung sharply on energy. That's exactly why the Fed watches shelter closely: unlike gasoline, it doesn't move with a ceasefire. Rent and owners' equivalent rent (what a homeowner would pay to rent their own home) are the two subcomponents BLS uses to build the shelter index; exact monthly percentages for those two are in BLS Table 7, linked below.
 </div>
</section>

<section>
 <h2>Mortgage rates</h2>
 <div class="stat-grid">
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">30-year fixed rate, current</div>
 <div class="value up">6.55%</div>
 <div class="period">week of July 16, 2026 (Freddie Mac)</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">30-year fixed, one year ago</div>
 <div class="value down">6.75%</div>
 <div class="period">week of July 2025</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">15-year fixed rate, current</div>
 <div class="value up">5.93%</div>
 <div class="period">week of July 16, 2026</div>
 </div>
 </div>
 <div class="note card" style="margin-top:12px;">
 Mortgage rates are a market price, not a government-set inflation index, but Freddie Mac's weekly survey is the standard benchmark and is often paired with CPI shelter data to gauge housing affordability. Rates ticked up 6 basis points this week to their highest level since August 2025, even as inventory keeps rising and Freddie Mac's chief economist described homebuying conditions as "modestly improving." Freddie Mac's next weekly update is due July 23, 2026.
 </div>
 <div class="source-card card" style="margin-top:12px;">
