</section>

<section>
 <h2>Energy &amp; fuel costs</h2>
 <p class="subnote">12-month percent change, not seasonally adjusted, from BLS CPI news releases.</p>
 <table class="trend-table card">
 <tr>
 <th>Measure</th><th>April 2026</th><th>May 2026</th><th>June 2026</th><th>Trend</th>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">Energy (overall)</td>
 <td>17.9%</td><td>23.5%</td><td>15.7%</td>
 <td class="arrow-down">↓ reversed sharply</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">Gasoline</td>
 <td>28.4%</td><td>40.5%</td><td>26.7%</td>
 <td class="arrow-down">↓ reversed sharply</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">Electricity</td>
 <td>—</td><td>5.9%</td><td>4.0%</td>
 <td class="arrow-down">↓ easing</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">Natural gas</td>
 <td>—</td><td>3.0%</td><td>3.0%</td>
 <td class="arrow-flat">↔ moderate</td>
 </tr>
 </table>
 <div class="note warn card" style="margin-top:12px;">
 Energy swung hard in both directions this spring and early summer. The Iran conflict pushed gasoline up as high as 40.5% year-over-year by May; a ceasefire in late June reopened the Strait of Hormuz and reversed most of that spike — gasoline fell 9.7% for the month in June alone (seasonally adjusted), cutting the annual rate to 26.7%. That's the largest 1-month gasoline price drop since August 2022. Energy overall dropped from +23.5% to +15.7% in a single month, and the entire monthly CPI decline in June was driven by this reversal. Electricity rose 4.0% and natural gas rose 3.0% year-over-year through June.
 </div>
</section>

<section>
 <h2>Health insurance &amp; medical care</h2>
 <div class="stat-grid">
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Medical care CPI, 12-mo</div>
 <div class="value down">2.0%</div>
 <div class="period">June 2026, all urban consumers</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Medical care services, 12-mo</div>
 <div class="value up">3.6%</div>
 <div class="period">May 2026 (latest subcomponent detail)</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Medical care commodities, 12-mo</div>
 <div class="value down">-1.8%</div>
 <div class="period">May 2026 (mostly Rx drugs)</div>
 </div>
 </div>
 <div class="note card" style="margin-top:12px;">
 Medical care cooled notably in June, down to 2.0% year-over-year from 2.6% in May — the index actually fell on the month, alongside apparel, communication, and used cars. That's now the lowest medical care reading of 2026 so far.
 </div>
 <div class="note warn card" style="margin-top:12px;">
 <strong>Important methodology note:</strong> BLS does not price health insurance policies directly. It uses an "indirect method" — measuring insurers' retained earnings (premiums minus benefits paid) rather than premium prices themselves — because it can't reliably control for changes in plan quality across insurers. There is no standalone monthly "health insurance inflation rate" published the way there is for, say, groceries or gasoline. Health insurance is folded into the broader "medical care services" category above.
 </div>
 <div class="source-card card" style="margin-top:12px;">
 <a href="https://www.bls.gov/cpi/factsheets/medical-care.htm" target="_blank">BLS — How CPI Measures Medical Care &amp; Health Insurance</a>
 <div class="desc">Explains the indirect pricing method for health insurance and why Medicaid isn't included in the CPI at all.</div>
 <span class="tag">bls.gov</span>
 </div>
</section>

