 <span class="tag">cms.gov</span>
 </div>
</section>

<section>
 <h2>Medicaid</h2>
 <div class="stat-grid">
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">National Medicaid spending, 2024</div>
 <div class="value up">+6.6%</div>
 <div class="period">grew to $931.7 billion (CMS NHE)</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Spending per enrollee, 2024</div>
 <div class="value up">+16.6%</div>
 <div class="period">largest of any payer type</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Share of national health spending</div>
 <div class="value up">18%</div>
 <div class="period">2024</div>
 </div>
 </div>
 <div class="note warn card" style="margin-top:12px;">
 <strong>Medicaid is not part of the CPI.</strong> BLS explicitly excludes Medicaid from CPI medical-care pricing because enrollees generally make no out-of-pocket payment to participate — there's no consumer "price" to track. What's shown here instead is CMS's National Health Expenditure (NHE) data, which measures total program spending growth, not a price index. Spending growth reflects both prices and how much care people used, so it isn't directly comparable to a CPI percentage.
 </div>
 <div class="source-card card" style="margin-top:12px;">
 <a href="https://www.cms.gov/data-research/statistics-trends-and-reports/national-health-expenditure-data/nhe-fact-sheet" target="_blank">CMS — National Health Expenditure Fact Sheet</a>
 <div class="desc">Official annual spending totals and growth rates by payer (Medicare, Medicaid, private insurance, out-of-pocket).</div>
 <span class="tag">cms.gov</span>
 </div>
</section>

<section>
 <h2>Unemployment</h2>
 <p class="subnote">Seasonally adjusted U-3 unemployment rate, from BLS Employment Situation reports.</p>
 <table class="trend-table card">
 <tr>
 <th>Measure</th><th>April 2026</th><th>May 2026</th><th>June 2026</th><th>Trend</th>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">Unemployment rate (U-3)</td>
 <td>4.3%</td><td>4.3%</td><td>4.2%</td>
 <td class="arrow-down">↓ slight improvement</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">Labor force participation</td>
 <td>—</td><td>—</td><td>61.5%</td>
 <td class="arrow-down">↓ falling</td>
 </tr>
 <tr>
 <td class="metric">Nonfarm payroll growth</td>
 <td>+148K</td><td>+129K</td><td>+57K</td>
 <td class="arrow-down">↓ slowing sharply</td>
 </tr>
 </table>
 <div class="stat-grid" style="margin-top:14px;">
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Initial jobless claims, weekly</div>
 <div class="value down">208,000</div>
 <div class="period">week ending July 11, 2026 (DOL)</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">Change from prior week</div>
 <div class="value down">-8,000</div>
 <div class="period">lowest in over two months</div>
 </div>
 </div>
 <div class="note warn card" style="margin-top:12px;">
 The June dip to 4.2% looks like good news, but BLS data show it was driven mainly by 720,000 people leaving the labor force — not by stronger hiring. Job growth itself slowed sharply, from 148,000 in April to just 57,000 in June, and April/May were revised down by a combined 74,000. Labor force participation fell to 61.5%, the lowest since March 2021. The one bright spot in the most current data: weekly jobless claims fell to 208,000 for the week ending July 11 — well below forecasts and the lowest reading in over two months — suggesting layoffs remain low even as hiring has cooled.
 </div>
