<section>
 <h2>Producer prices (PPI) — a leading indicator</h2>
 <p class="subnote">Measures prices businesses receive for goods/services before they reach consumers — often a preview of future CPI.</p>
 <div class="stat-grid">
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">PPI, final demand, 12-mo</div>
 <div class="value down">5.5%</div>
 <div class="period">June 2026, down from 6.5% peak in May</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">PPI, monthly change</div>
 <div class="value down">-0.3%</div>
 <div class="period">largest drop since a 0.6% rise in May</div>
 </div>
 <div class="stat card">
 <div class="label">PPI energy, monthly</div>
 <div class="value down">-6.4%</div>
 <div class="period">gasoline at wholesale: -12.0%</div>
 </div>
 </div>
 <div class="note card" style="margin-top:12px;">
 PPI tends to lead CPI by a month or two, since it captures price changes before they're passed on to consumers. June's sharp pullback — echoing the same Strait of Hormuz ceasefire that pulled down consumer gasoline prices — suggests the wholesale pipeline pressure that's been pushing inflation higher may be easing, which is a modest positive sign for July and August CPI. Goods prices fell 1.4%, the largest drop since July 2022, though services PPI still rose 0.2%.
 </div>
 <div class="source-card card" style="margin-top:12px;">
 <a href="https://www.bls.gov/ppi/" target="_blank">BLS — Producer Price Index (PPI)</a>
 <div class="desc">Monthly wholesale/producer-level price data, often a leading indicator for future consumer inflation.</div>
 <span class="tag">bls.gov</span>
 </div>
</section>

